data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the EF Core Cosmos DB provider allowed synchronous LINQ operators (`ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()`) via an explicit opt-in that suppressed the sync-over-async warning. **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** The Cosmos provider no longer offers sync API surface — calling sync methods throws `InvalidOperationException` at runtime.

## What You Need to Change

Convert all synchronous calls to their async equivalents:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()`
- `First()` → `FirstAsync()`
- `Count()` → `CountAsync()`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()`
- All callers up the stack must propagate `async`/`await`

This is a breaking change with no workaround — the Cosmos provider is async-only in EF Core 11.
